{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_21","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '700,845p' src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc; sed -n '1120,1148p' test/yang/clojure_test.clj; rg -n '\"'^'\"\\\\(deftest.*(common-call|tail|try|thread|library.*fn|shadow)' test/yang/clojure_test.clj; rg -n 'invoker|callable|raw|select-library' collab/yang-clojure --glob '*findings.md' | tail -25\"","aggregated_output":"    :else form))\n\n\n(defn- call-kind\n  [env head]\n  (cond\n    (keyword? head) :keyword\n    (not (symbol? head)) :guest\n    (macro-call? env (::macro-env env) head) :macro\n    (native-name? env head) (if (contains? (adapters env) head) :adapted :native)\n    :else :guest))\n\n\n(def ^:private lowered-specials\n  #{'if 'let 'do 'loop 'recur 'throw 'try 'quote 'fn 'and 'or})\n\n\n(declare failing? expand-control)\n\n\n(def ^:private control-heads\n  \"case and the standard control forms: compiler forms that lower to\n   if/let/do, so the outcome pass sees through them like those.\"\n  #{'case 'cond 'when 'if-let 'if-some 'when-let 'when-some '->})\n\n\n(defn- any-failing?\n  [env forms]\n  (boolean (some #(failing? env %) forms)))\n\n\n(defn- check-macro-operands!\n  \"A macro call is opaque to the lowering pass, so an operand that may fail\n   would escape the early return: refuse it, naming the macro.\"\n  [env head ops]\n  (when (any-failing? env ops)\n    (reject! (str \"Macro call \" head \" has an operand that may fail; \"\n                  \"the outcome model cannot lower it\")\n             {:macro head})))\n\n\n(defn- scoped-failing?\n  \"Whether a sequence of [target init] pairs or the body after them can fail,\n   each init seeing the targets before it.\"\n  [env pairs body]\n  (loop [env env, ps pairs]\n    (if (empty? ps)\n      (any-failing? env body)\n      (let [[target init] (first ps)]\n        (or (failing? env init)\n            (recur (with-locals env (binder-symbols target)) (rest ps)))))))\n\n\n(defn- failing?\n  \"True when evaluating `form` can produce an error outcome: a throw, or a\n   call that returns an outcome (a guest call or an adapted primitive).\"\n  [env form]\n  (cond\n    (seq? form)\n    (if (empty? form)\n      false\n      (let [[head & ops] form]\n        (cond\n          (contains? #{'quote 'fn} head) false\n          (= 'throw head) true\n          (= 'try head) true\n          (= 'let head) (let [[bindings & body] ops]\n                          (scoped-failing? env (partition 2 (expand-bindings bindings env)) body))\n          ;; the raw outcome of one guest call is a value, not a failure\n          (= 'yin.k/outcome-of head) (do (when-not (and (= 1 (count ops)) (seq? (first ops)))\n                                           (reject! \"yin.k/outcome-of takes one call\" {:form form}))\n                                         (when (any-failing? env (first ops))\n                                           (reject! \"yin.k/outcome-of operands cannot fail\"\n                                                    {:form form}))\n                                         false)\n          (= 'dsg__let head) (let [[bindings & body] ops]\n                               (scoped-failing? env (partition 2 bindings) body))\n          (= 'loop head) (let [[bindings & body] ops]\n                           (scoped-failing? env (partition 2 bindings) body))\n          (contains? control-heads head) (failing? env (expand-control env head ops))\n          (contains? lowered-specials head) (any-failing? env ops)\n          :else (case (call-kind env head)\n                  (:guest :adapted) true\n                  :macro (do (check-macro-operands! env head ops) false)\n                  (any-failing? env ops)))))\n    (or (vector? form) (set? form)) (any-failing? env form)\n    (map? form) (any-failing? env (concat (keys form) (vals form)))\n    :else false))\n\n\n(defn- atomic?\n  [form]\n  (or (symbol? form) (constant-form? form)))\n\n\n(declare lower-tail)\n\n\n(defn- bind-outcome\n  \"Bind `outcome`, return it as is when it is an error, else continue with\n   its payload.\"\n  [env outcome k]\n  (let [o (gen-sym env)]\n    (list 'dsg__let [o outcome]\n          (list 'if (list 'data/content= (list 'data/nth o 0) error-tag)\n                o\n                (k (list 'data/nth o 1))))))\n\n\n(defn- lower-operand\n  \"Continue with a simple form for `form`: the form itself when it cannot\n   fail, else the payload of its outcome.\"\n  [env form k]\n  (if (failing? env form)\n    (bind-outcome env (lower-tail env form) k)\n    (k form)))\n\n\n(defn- lower-operands\n  \"Continue with simple forms for `forms`, evaluated left to right: a form\n   before a may-fail one is bound first so the order of effects survives.\"\n  [env forms k]\n  (letfn [(go\n            [fs acc]\n            (if (empty? fs)\n              (k acc)\n              (let [f (first fs), more (rest fs)]\n                (cond\n                  (atomic? f) (go more (conj acc f))\n                  (failing? env f) (lower-operand env f (fn [v] (go more (conj acc v))))\n                  (any-failing? env more) (let [t (gen-sym env)]\n                                            (list 'dsg__let [t f] (go more (conj acc t))))\n                  :else (go more (conj acc f))))))]\n    (go (seq forms) [])))\n\n\n(defn- lower-let\n  \"`pairs` are plain [symbol init] pairs.\"\n  [env pairs body]\n  (letfn [(go\n            [env ps]\n            (if (empty? ps)\n              (lower-tail env (body-expression body))\n              (let [[x init] (first ps)]\n                (lower-operand env init\n                               (fn [v]\n        ((host 'probe\n               [['note\n                 (fn [x] (swap! effect-log conj x) x)\n                 (vm/primitive-profile 'note :pure [1] #{} :none)]])))))\n\n\n(def ^:private outcome-opts\n  {:convention :arg-vector, :outcomes true\n   :native (set (keys vm/primitives))\n   :adapters {'data/nth 'portable/nth}})\n\n\n(defn- outcome-ast\n  [forms]\n  (yang/compile-program forms true outcome-opts))\n\n\n(defn- run-outcome\n  \"The raw outcome the last form of `forms` returns.\"\n  [forms]\n  (reset! effect-log [])\n  (-> (tu/make-observer-session (tu/create-vm {:modules outcome-modules}))\n      (tu/queue-ast! (outcome-ast forms))\n      tu/run-session\n      :consumer\n      vm/value))\n\n\n(defn- run-profile-outcome\n857:(deftest test-non-tail-recursion-controls\n973:(deftest test-loop-tail-audit\n1182:(deftest test-common-call-descriptor-publication\n1198:(deftest test-common-call-hand-constructed-descriptor\n1218:(deftest test-common-call-profile-eligibility\n1245:(deftest test-common-call-invocation-source-admission\n1267:(deftest test-common-call-invocation-operand-order\n1294:(deftest test-common-call-shadowed-helpers\n1317:(deftest test-common-call-source-admission\n1351:(deftest test-common-call-invalid-continuation-refuses\n1366:(deftest test-common-call-rejects-named-arguments\n1521:(deftest test-outcome-common-call-depth\n1710:(deftest test-case-tail-position\n2254:(deftest test-portable-try-outcomes\n2274:(deftest test-guest-callable-collections-and-threading\ncollab/yang-clojure/1793296786225-reviewer-hardening2.glm-5.3.findings.md:14:**P3 | src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:1163-1166** | Callable collections route through `invoke-value` only when the operator is a **local** or a **non-symbol** (`(or (contains? (locals env) operator) (not (symbol? operator)))`). A top-level defined collection — `(def v [1 2]) (v 0)` — compiles to a plain `:application` and errors at runtime instead of indexing, diverging from Clojure. Untested boundary of the \"callable collections\" admission (test/yang/clojure_test.clj:2049 uses only `let`-bound collections). | Fix: record as a known boundary in the library notes, or extend the route to operators in `::defined`.\ncollab/yang-clojure/1793309500000-reviewer-adopt-s1.glm-5.3.findings.md:10:**P1 | src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:1057-1059 + src/cljc/yang/core/call.cljc:33-40 | Ordinary map writes forge callables; the data-map boundary fixture is missing.** Descriptors are plain immutable maps built by guest `assoc`, and `yang.call/eligible?` is purely structural (any map with non-nil `:yang.call/entry`). Probe: `(let [h (assoc {} :yang.call/entry (get f :yang.call/entry))] (h 21))` → `[:yin.k/ok 42]` — an ordinary `assoc` mints a working callable. The ruling's Additional obligations state \"A source map containing `:yang.call/entry` remains data unless constructed by the pinned callable constructor/projection. Ordinary map writes must not overwrite or forge the runtime entry\", and the 8.10.11 acceptance gate requires exercising \"source data with an entry-spelled property remains data\" (yang.antlr.md:4029-4031). No negative fixture exists; `test-common-call-hand-constructed-descriptor` (test/yang/clojure_test.clj:1183-1191) enshrines the opposite admission. Mitigating context: the orchestrator prompt (collab/1793338209376-…prompt.md item 5) explicitly directed the hand-constructed fixture \"as the spec's own illustration shows\". This needs architect arbitration: either seal/admit via a pinned constructor, or record the structural admission as this profile's defined projection (yang.antlr.md:3590-3592 gives cover) and add the boundary fixtures (non-callable entry → stays data; forged/assoc'd descriptor → refused).\ncollab/yang-clojure/1793309500000-reviewer-adopt-s1.glm-5.3.findings.md:12:**P2 | src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:1163-1169 | A2's \"a missing entry means not callable\" is not enforced for source applications.** The routing is `(if (yang.call/eligible? callee) common-call direct-call)` — a value-structural exemption (\"anything without an entry applies directly\"), not dsg__/private-scoped. `:yang.call/not-callable` is unreachable from compiled source calls; an entry-less foreign callable (e.g. a pre-migration py/js cell, which is a map) surfaces as a raw VM defect — probe: `(let [x 5] (x 1))` → host \"Cannot apply non-function\" — rather than the qualified condition. The reachable closure cases (adapters, macro lambdas, continuations) do work, and the orchestrator prompt item 2 sanctioned \"plain non-descriptor closures (internal, private, pre-adopted program values) apply directly as today\", which is broader than the architect's \"internal compiler-generated calls may remain private\". Fix: record the deviation with the architect, or scope the direct branch (e.g. compiler-generated callees only) so missing-entry foreign values get the protocol condition.\ncollab/yang-clojure/1793309500000-reviewer-adopt-s1.glm-5.3.findings.md:16:**P3 | src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:1165-1169 | Silent bad-value fallback when the captured continuation is not callable.** `(if (data/callable? escape) …common/direct… escape)` returns the continuation itself as the call's value instead of refusing. Unreachable on both current lanes (walker and semantic both evaluate `%capture` to a callable), but the branch should refuse, not yield a continuation as a value.\ncollab/yang-clojure/1793314000000-engineer-adopt-fix2.kimi-k3.1-pro.findings.md:7:  - Source-resolved calls require admitted descriptors; scalars, ordinary maps, malformed descriptors, and foreign values return `:yang.call/not-callable`.\ncollab/yang-clojure/1793391000000-reviewer-adopt-n1n2.glm-5.3.findings.md:4:Third confirmation round, narrowly scoped to the two P2s I raised in r2, against fix commit `12feb7d1` (`git diff abbfb998..12feb7d1`, re-read from the diff, not assumed). All probes re-ran read-only on the JVM lane through the test namespace's own runners (`run-outcome`, `run-profile-outcome`, `walker-run`, `semantic-run`, `outcome-ast`) plus `#'yang.clojure/admit-call-descriptor` for the eligibility arms; no source files edited, nothing committed. Probe-honesty note: my r2 report quoted the probes with a bare `raise` operand; this round established that source-spelled fn/defn values are descriptor **maps**, not applicable values, in this lane — the verdict-bearing probes below therefore spell raise exactly the way the corpus and the compiler's own bridges do (an applicable `probe/note` host splice / raw lam), and the source-spelled-raise behavior is reported separately as informational.\ncollab/yang-clojure/1793391000000-reviewer-adopt-n1n2.glm-5.3.findings.md:8:Ruling (kimi r5): witness-gate both operators at the compile branch like `yang.call/eligible?`; qualified `:yang.call/not-callable` for every unadmitted callee; no shared-runtime change. Verified in place: the new branch at `clojure.cljc:1298-1318` sits before the `native-name?` fall-through, checks `dsg__admitted?` on `dsg__invocation_candidate`, routes the success arm through the shared runtime and the refusal arm through `yang.call/transfer` + `condition :yang.call/not-callable` + callee evidence. `src/cljc/yang/core/call.cljc` is untouched by the fix (diff lists only the manifests, `clojure.cljc`, and tests) — the no-shared-runtime-change ruling is honored.\ncollab/yang-clojure/1793391000000-reviewer-adopt-n1n2.glm-5.3.findings.md:13::probe-copied-invoke       [:yin.k/ok #:yang.call{:reason :yang.call/not-callable, :data #:yang.call{:callee #:yang.call{:entry {:type :closure}}}}]   ; r2: [:yin.k/ok 42] — the forged callee RAN\ncollab/yang-clojure/1793391000000-reviewer-adopt-n1n2.glm-5.3.findings.md:14::probe-copied-invoke-with  [:yin.k/ok #:yang.call{:reason :yang.call/not-callable, :data #:yang.call{:callee #:yang.call{:entry {:type :closure}}}}]\ncollab/yang-clojure/1793391000000-reviewer-adopt-n1n2.glm-5.3.findings.md:15::probe-scalar-invoke       [:yin.k/ok #:yang.call{:reason :yang.call/not-callable, :data #:yang.call{:callee #:yang.call{:entry 5}}}]                    ; r2: raw host \"Cannot apply non-function\"\ncollab/yang-clojure/1793391000000-reviewer-adopt-n1n2.glm-5.3.findings.md:16::probe-scalar-invoke-with  [:yin.k/ok #:yang.call{:reason :yang.call/not-callable, :data #:yang.call{:callee #:yang.call{:entry 5}}}]\ncollab/yang-clojure/1793391000000-reviewer-adopt-n1n2.glm-5.3.findings.md:17::probe-guestfn-invoke      [:yin.k/ok #:yang.call{:reason :yang.call/not-callable, :data #:yang.call{:callee #:yang.call{:entry {…full guest-fn descriptor…}}}}]  ; r2: raw host defect\ncollab/yang-clojure/1793391000000-reviewer-adopt-n1n2.glm-5.3.findings.md:26::elig false :walker/:semantic  :invoke [:yin.k/ok #:yang.call{:reason :yang.call/not-callable, :data {:callee callable}}]  :invoke-with (same)  :operator [:yin.k/ok false]\ncollab/yang-clojure/1793391000000-reviewer-adopt-n1n2.glm-5.3.findings.md:32::alias-forged-invoke    [:yin.k/ok #:yang.call{:reason :yang.call/not-callable, :data #:yang.call{:callee #:yang.call{:entry {:type :closure}}}}]\ncollab/yang-clojure/1793391000000-reviewer-adopt-n1n2.glm-5.3.findings.md:33::alias-forged-scalar    [:yin.k/ok #:yang.call{:reason :yang.call/not-callable, :data #:yang.call{:callee #:yang.call{:entry 5}}}]\ncollab/yang-clojure/1793391000000-reviewer-adopt-n1n2.glm-5.3.findings.md:38:**P3 | informational, pre-existing, non-blocking | source-spelled raise operands are not callable values.** `(fn [c] c)` or `(defn raise …)` in the raise operand compiles to a descriptor map (`compile-av-fn` → `compile-call-descriptor`, clojure.cljc:1116-1118); the shared runtime's `(if (data/callable? raise) … (raise …))` else-branch then applies the value it just decided is not callable (call.cljc:118,142) → host `{:reason :not-applicable, :kind :map}` \"Cannot apply non-function\". This crashes the **positive control identically** and `call.cljc` is byte-identical at `abbfb998`, so it is a pre-existing property of the surface (the invoke raise operand is profile-mediated: host fns and raw lams — the fixtures' `profile-raise` splices and the compiler's own bridges at clojure.cljc:1194,1234 — are the spellings that have ever worked), not a regression from `12feb7d1`; the new refusal path inherits the same class only for raise spellings that never worked anywhere on this surface. Recorded for the architect's list (the non-callable else-branch applying its own raise is the odd part); does not gate sign-off under r5, which pinned \"no change to `yang.core.call` is required\". Same class: under-arity `(yang.call/invoke h [21])` (below the branch's `(<= 4 …)` guard, pre-fix compilation unchanged) → host defect; over-arity tolerated (42) — both identical in class at the parent commit.\ncollab/yang-clojure/1793391000000-reviewer-adopt-n1n2.glm-5.3.findings.md:69:**Ready for Architect sign-off.** Both P2s from r2 are closed with probe evidence at `12feb7d1`: the protocol's front door now refuses every unadmitted callee with the qualified `:yang.call/not-callable` condition through both operators (including the alias route), genuinely admitted callees — static and dynamic-eligibility — still work on both lanes, the shared runtime is untouched, and the closure gate test is green with the manifests regenerated. The one P3 (source-spelled raise operands being non-callable values, a pre-existing property that affects admitted callees identically and predates this fix) is recorded for the architect's backlog and does not gate this adoption.\ncollab/yang-clojure/1793386000000-reviewer-adopt-fix-r2.glm-5.3.findings.md:4:Confirmation round against `abbfb998` (branch rebased onto `63674368`; everything re-read from `git diff 63674368..abbfb998`, not assumed from the pre-rebase review). I re-ran my own s1 probes for each arbitrated item, probed the eligibility follow-up, swept the new fix code adversarially, and re-ran the required suites. All probes ran read-only on the JVM lane through the test namespace's own runners (`run-outcome`, `walker-run`, `semantic-run`, `run-library`, and `#'yang.clojure/admit-call-descriptor` for UAST-level construction); no source files edited, no commit made. Probe-honesty note: two of my early probe constructions were themselves broken (keyword vectors `[:go 3 0]` read as keyword calls, and `conj` on a quoted list reordering forms); the corrected runs below are the ones I stand behind, and one earlier \"crash\" (raw-entry application) was my harness passing forms to an AST runner — the corrected probe refuses cleanly.\ncollab/yang-clojure/1793386000000-reviewer-adopt-fix-r2.glm-5.3.findings.md:10:**P1-2 assoc-forged descriptors — CLOSED as probed; boundary re-opened at the protocol front door (N1 below).** Ruling (a): constructor/profile-produced descriptors distinguished from ordinary source maps. Admission witness: `admit-call-descriptor` (clojure.cljc:1072-1089) stores a closure captured over the canonical descriptor; `dsg__admitted?` reconstructs and compares via `data/content=` and never applies a source-supplied predicate; the gate wraps every source application (clojure.cljc:1239). Application-syntax probes: forged `(assoc {} :yang.call/entry (get f :yang.call/entry))` → `:yang.call/not-callable` (s1: `[:yin.k/ok 42]`); entry-swap f→g → refused; `(assoc f :extra :forged)` → refused; copy entry+admission keys → refused; `(apply h [21])` → refused; entry-spelled data with a non-callable entry stays data (`(yang.call/eligible? {:yang.call/entry 5})` → `[:yin.k/ok false]`). Negative fixtures exist (`test-common-call-source-admission`, 10 shapes × 2 wrappers). **But** the same forged map handed to `yang.call/invoke` runs — see N1.\ncollab/yang-clojure/1793386000000-reviewer-adopt-fix-r2.glm-5.3.findings.md:12:**P2-1 direct-apply branch — CLOSED.** Ruling (c): every source-resolved application through common admission; missing entry means `:yang.call/not-callable`. Probes: `(let [x 5] (x 1))` → `[:yin.k/error {:yang.call/reason :yang.call/not-callable, :data {:yang.call/callee 5}}]` (s1: host crash); ordinary map and entry-less foreign value (`{:py/type :function :code :legacy}`) → same qualified condition; refusal also in tail position; non-symbol operators are gated too (`((if true f g) 21)` → 42 works, `((if false f 5) 21)` and `((yang.call/entry f) {})` → not-callable). Only generated `dsg__` implementation calls apply closures directly, as ruled.\ncollab/yang-clojure/1793386000000-reviewer-adopt-fix-r2.glm-5.3.findings.md:22:`(yang.call/eligible?)` as a source operator compiles to admitted-first-then-predicate (clojure.cljc:1286-1296); `fn?` in the guest library consults it (clojure.cljc:2146-2148). Probes via `#'yang.clojure/admit-call-descriptor` (the sanctioned profile construction path), both lanes: descriptor with `:yang.call/eligible?` → `true`: call works `[:yin.k/ok :called]`; predicate `false`: call refused `:yang.call/not-callable`; operator form honors the predicate (`true`/`false`); plain source fns still admitted (`[:yin.k/ok [true 42]]`). Matches `test-common-call-profile-eligibility`. Value-position references do NOT leak a structural answer: `(def p yang.call/eligible?) (p h)` → false and library `(fn? h)` → false for the forged `h` — consistent with the gated operator form (the library defn compiles through the same branch).\ncollab/yang-clojure/1793386000000-reviewer-adopt-fix-r2.glm-5.3.findings.md:26:**P2 | src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:1299 (native-name? branch, via :588) + src/cljc/yang/core/call.cljc:22,33-40 | Admission bypass at the protocol front door: `yang.call/invoke`/`invoke-with` skip the witness gate.** Namespaced operators are `native-name?`-true, so source calls to `yang.call/invoke` and `yang.call/invoke-with` compile through the direct native application branch and never pass `dsg__admitted?`; the runtime `yang.call/eligible?` they consult is structural **by design** (\"Descriptor projection remains structural after callable admission\", call.cljc:22). Probes: `(let [h (assoc {} :yang.call/entry (get f :yang.call/entry))] (yang.call/invoke h [21] [] raise))` → **`[:yin.k/ok 42]`** — the forged callee runs (same via `invoke-with`); control with the real `f` → 42, so the surface itself is legitimate and corpus-exercised. Sharper shapes: guest-fn or scalar entries forged the same way → raw host **\"Cannot apply non-function\"** — a defect, not a condition — the exact class ruling (c) said must not surface. Mitigations found: admitted-but-ineligible profiles still refuse through the runtime predicate hook, and the copied entry only re-runs its original body (no privilege escalation demonstrated — the forged callable is behaviorally the original). Still, ruling (a) states the boundary absolutely (\"Copying :yang.call/entry into it must not make it callable\"), and the copy IS callable here. Fix (either): extend the clojure.cljc:1286 compile-branch pattern to `invoke`/`invoke-with` so their callee operand is witness-gated (smallest change: one more branch case), or record with the architect that the protocol's own front door is the sanctioned profile-level construction channel, with a ledger row plus fixtures pinning exactly what it admits and that non-closure entries refuse with a qualified condition rather than a host defect.\ncollab/yang-clojure/1793389000000-engineer-adopt-fix3.kimi-k3.1-pro.findings.md:6:  - Witness-gated `yang.call/invoke` and `invoke-with`; forged callees now return `:yang.call/not-callable`.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
